	url = {https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/9780470891056.ch11},
	doi = {10.1002/9780470891056.ch11},
	abstract = {The idea of using the propensity score method in survey methodology was introduced by Harris Interactive. Schonlau et al. elaborate on the technique of the propensity score method. The methods presented in this chapter use response probabilities, or response propensities, for the nonresponse bias adjustment without the use of a reference survey. The chapter deals with response propensity, and outlines the adjustment weighting approach to nonresponse and the usual methods to estimate population characteristics based on a survey with nonresponse. These methods can be modified to incorporate response propensities. A number of modifications to nonresponse adjustment methods are described that use response propensities. The chapter describes how response propensities are estimated. It also explains how the balancing property of the response propensity stratification is checked. Different response propensity methods are finally applied to the General Population Survey (GPS).},
